WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:02.055
Today, we're building a number guessing

2
00:00:02.075 --> 00:00:02.335
game.

3
00:00:02.816 --> 00:00:04.379
It's a classic first program.

4
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.964
In this game, the program picks a secret

5
00:00:07.004 --> 00:00:09.047
number, and the player tries to find it.

6
00:00:09.608 --> 00:00:11.492
We'll use everything we've learned so far:

7
00:00:11.973 --> 00:00:13.836
print statements, variables, if-else

8
00:00:13.856 --> 00:00:14.878
conditions, and loops.

9
00:00:15.459 --> 00:00:17.162
It's going to be fun!

10
00:00:17.162 --> 00:00:19.244
To pick a secret number, we'll use the

11
00:00:19.324 --> 00:00:20.105
random module.

12
00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:22.668
This module can choose a number for us.

13
00:00:23.269 --> 00:00:24.390
By using random.seed(),

14
00:00:25.411 --> 00:00:27.333
we can make the choice repeatable, which

15
00:00:27.413 --> 00:00:28.594
is perfect for testing.

16
00:00:29.055 --> 00:00:31.778
And with random.randint(1, 10), we can get

17
00:00:31.818 --> 00:00:33.279
a whole number from 1 to 10.

18
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:35.281
Let's look at that in a picture.

19
00:00:35.642 --> 00:00:36.543
Here's how it works.

20
00:00:37.183 --> 00:00:38.685
First, we import random.

21
00:00:39.266 --> 00:00:40.927
Then we set the seed to 7 and

22
00:00:40.987 --> 00:00:41.969
pick our secret number.

23
00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:44.732
Finally, we print a message to confirm the

24
00:00:44.772 --> 00:00:46.153
number is chosen.

25
00:00:46.572 --> 00:00:48.595
Next, we need to check if the player's

26
00:00:48.635 --> 00:00:49.536
guess is correct.

27
00:00:50.077 --> 00:00:51.699
We can compare the guess to the secret

28
00:00:51.759 --> 00:00:54.143
number using the == operator.

29
00:00:54.743 --> 00:00:56.646
If they match, we print a win message.

30
00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:58.989
If not, we print a hint to try

31
00:00:59.049 --> 00:00:59.270
again.

32
00:00:59.791 --> 00:01:01.994
For example, if our secret number is 6

33
00:01:02.154 --> 00:01:04.337
and the user guesses 4, we can see

34
00:01:04.377 --> 00:01:05.719
how the if-else statement works.

35
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.282
It checks the guess and gives feedback

36
00:01:08.363 --> 00:01:09.424
accordingly.

37
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.602
Now, let's talk about the game loop.

38
00:01:12.123 --> 00:01:14.246
Since a program can't type, we give it

39
00:01:14.286 --> 00:01:15.687
a list of guesses to work with.

40
00:01:16.228 --> 00:01:17.790
The for loop will try each guess in

41
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:19.572
order, just like a player would.

42
00:01:20.173 --> 00:01:21.895
The if-else statement decides what to

43
00:01:21.955 --> 00:01:22.896
print for each guess.

44
00:01:23.437 --> 00:01:25.179
For instance, if we have a list of

45
00:01:25.239 --> 00:01:27.662
guesses, the program will check each one

46
00:01:27.743 --> 00:01:29.665
against the secret number and respond

47
00:01:29.725 --> 00:01:30.246
accordingly.

48
00:01:30.286 --> 00:01:33.690
This makes the game interactive and fun!

49
00:01:33.991 --> 00:01:35.694
So, what did your program just do?

50
00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:37.977
It picked a secret number and stored it

51
00:01:38.057 --> 00:01:38.678
in a variable.

52
00:01:39.339 --> 00:01:41.362
Then, it looped over the guesses one by

53
00:01:41.402 --> 00:01:41.683
one.

54
00:01:42.263 --> 00:01:44.287
For each guess, it made a decision and

55
00:01:44.347 --> 00:01:45.188
printed the result.

56
00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:47.732
This is what a real program looks like

57
00:01:47.732 --> 00:01:49.975
— it has the same structure as the

58
00:01:50.035 --> 00:01:51.898
software you use every day.

59
00:01:52.452 --> 00:01:54.294
Now that you've built your game, where do

60
00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:54.935
you go from here?

61
00:01:55.536 --> 00:01:57.759
You could change the range to 1-100 and

62
00:01:58.239 --> 00:01:59.160
add more guesses.

63
00:01:59.821 --> 00:02:01.463
You might also want to count the attempts

64
00:02:01.523 --> 00:02:03.866
with a variable that increases by 1 each

65
00:02:03.906 --> 00:02:04.587
loop.

66
00:02:04.607 --> 00:02:06.249
And when you run Python on your own

67
00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.652
computer, you can swap the guess list for

68
00:02:08.693 --> 00:02:11.396
input() to make it truly interactive.

69
00:02:12.017 --> 00:02:13.819
The possibilities are endless!

